course: dictionary
lesson: 12543
title: Deterioration: getting worse
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Deterioration is a noun. It means the process of getting worse in quality, condition or health. When something is in deterioration, it is slowly becoming worse.
explain: «The old bridge showed a deterioration in its paint and steel.» Look at the clues: old, paint and steel. The bridge is getting worse. Try your meaning in the sentence: the bridge showed a process of getting worse in its paint. It still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: Scientists use deterioration for materials. «The rain caused rapid deterioration of the wooden fence.» Rapid means fast, so the wood broke down quickly, like rotting. Look for words such as in, of, rapid and sudden, because they often sit close to deterioration and give you clues.
explain: Doctors use it for people too. «The nurse noticed a sudden deterioration in the patient's health overnight.» Sudden means quick and unexpected, so the patient became worse very quickly. When you are not sure, check a dictionary.
fact: Deterioration means becoming worse, or the slow breaking down of materials.
fact: Common phrases are «deterioration in», «deterioration of», «rapid deterioration» and «a sudden deterioration».
fact: Deterioration is usually uncountable, so we say «deterioration», not «deteriorations».
fact: Rust and rot are examples of deterioration: metal rusts and wood rots over time.
fact: Deterioration is the opposite of improvement, which means getting better.
exercise: «The paint on the old boat showed deterioration after years in the salty sea.» What does deterioration mean here? | *Becoming worse, slowly breaking down | Getting brighter and newer | Turning into a song | Being sold to a museum
exercise: «There was a rapid deterioration in the weather, so the picnic was cancelled.» Which word tells you the change happened quickly? | picnic | *rapid | cancelled | weather
exercise: Dad says, «The car's brakes show deterioration, so we must fix them now.» Why does Dad want them fixed? | *Because they are getting worse and may not work well | Because they cost too much to keep | Because they make a lovely sound | Because they are shiny and new
test: What does a deterioration in someone's health mean? | Their health is getting better | They have gone on holiday | *Their health is getting worse | They are fast asleep
test: «Metal left out in the rain shows deterioration as it rusts.» What causes the deterioration here? | Someone painting the metal | A brand-new coat of paint | *Rust from rain slowly breaking down the metal | Sunshine making the metal shine
exam: Which sentence uses deterioration correctly? | The team made a deterioration in their training, so they played better | Deterioration is a colour of paint | The deterioration was a happy, lovely birthday party | *The roof showed a deterioration of its tiles after the storm
